implementationPlan.description:
vm-control.mjs seat-proof wiring, with rows for DoD 3 and 4.
(a) Export localRunnerHost({ platform, env, hostname }), mirroring the wrappers: on win32, COMPUTERNAME lowercased (moe-agent.ps1 Get-MoeLiveMarkerHost); elsewhere, os.hostname() lowercased with whitespace removed (moe-agent.sh live_marker_host).
(b) Export runnerProbe(record, { kill = process.kill, host = localRunnerHost(), platform = process.platform }), returning true, false or undefined. It returns undefined unless record.host === host (non-empty), record.platform === platform and the runner parses as /^([1-9][0-9]{0,9})@/. Then it calls kill(pid, 0): signal 0 only, an existence check that delivers nothing. Success or EPERM is true, ESRCH is false, anything else is undefined. A reused pid reads alive, so the VM is preserved, never deleted; document that residual.
(c) seatStateOf(call) calls call('moe.get_seat_liveness', { workerId }). This is CQ-M07g0's read-only tool, replying { workerId, registered, status, openAttempts }. Map the reply:
  - 'registered': registered is true and status !== 'DEAD';
  - 'attempt-open': openAttempts > 0;
  - 'gone': otherwise;
  - 'unknown': the reply is not an object, names another workerId, has a non-boolean registered, or has an openAttempts that is not a non-negative safe integer.
  A thrown call propagates to seatHoldReason's catch (seat-unknown).
(d) Extract cleanupReport's leaseHeld closure, unchanged, into leaseHeldOf(call): a fresh list_resources per decision, and an unreadable list counts as held. cleanupReport builds its owners map from attempt records only, and passes seatState: seatStateOf(call) and runnerAlive (default runnerProbe) to cleanupOwned.
(e) reconcileReport({ ledger, provider, call, runnerAlive = runnerProbe }) lists once and hands the same listing to reconcileOwned and reconcileSeats. It reports { reconciled: attempt then seat, unknown: attempt then seat, invalid, held, retired }. Each record lands in exactly one list, because the IDE parsers refuse a report that names an instance twice. Only seat records reach the daemon, so an attempt-only ledger never touches it.
(f) runVmControl passes call (deps.call ?? mcpCaller(project); it is lazy and reads daemon.json only when called) and deps.runnerAlive to reconcile as well as cleanup. Update the header comment.
Tests:
- fakeDaemon gains `seats`, answering moe.get_seat_liveness; an Error entry rejects.
- The VC-R1 and VC-O1 expectations gain held: [] and retired: []. VC-O1's exact CLI line ends ,"invalid":0,"held":[],"retired":[]}.
- VC-C3 (DoD 3). Seat records, all Running:
  - registered IDLE: seat-registered;
  - registered DEAD, 0 open, runner false, lease free: deleted, with its key files unlinked;
  - unregistered, 1 open: seat-attempt-open;
  - a malformed reply: seat-unknown;
  - gone, runner true: runner-alive;
  - gone, runner undefined: runner-unknown;
  - gone, runner false, lease held: job-lease-held;
  - unregistered, 0 open, runner false, free: deleted.
  Provider calls are only info/delete/info for the two deleted names, and list_resources runs only for records that reached the lease check. A second run with the daemon down preserves every remaining record as seat-unknown.
- VC-R2 (DoD 4):
  - a registered seat's 'launching' record: held and untouched, with no provider call;
  - gone, 'launching', absent: launching→absent;
  - gone, 'reserved', absent, with stray key files: retired, and both the record and the key files are gone;
  - gone, 'reserved', present: reserved→collision;
  - gone, 'owned', provider FAIL: unknown;
  - an attempt 'owned' record, absent: owned→absent, with no daemon call for it.
  A second run is idempotent.
- VC-P1. runnerProbe with a fake kill: a host or platform mismatch, a blank host or a malformed runner gives undefined and never calls kill. A plain return gives true, EPERM true, ESRCH false, any other code undefined. kill is always called with (pid, 0). One real call with `${process.pid}@x`, host localRunnerHost() and platform process.platform gives true; never signal another process. localRunnerHost maps win32 COMPUTERNAME 'DESK-1' to 'desk-1', and a linux hostname ' My Host ' to 'myhost'.
